{0 Generating notes: a worked example}

A small vault, six templates, and the [dune] rules that turn one into the
other. The JSON namespace the templates read is specified in
{!page-"template-context"}; this page is only about wiring it to a build.

The example is real: it lives in [pkg/oystermark/examples/vault-build/] and
builds with

{v
dune build @pkg/oystermark/examples/vault-build/vault-build
v}

{1 The tree}

The vault is a directory of its own, so that the build's own outputs are never
mistaken for notes:

{v
pkg/oystermark/examples/vault-build/
├── dune
├── shopping-stub.json
└── vault/
    ├── focaccia.md
    ├── folding.svg
    ├── kneading.md
    ├── sourdough.md
    ├── starter.md
    ├── health.md.jinja
    ├── hydration.md.jinja
    ├── index.md.jinja
    ├── plan.md.jinja
    ├── shopping.md.jinja
    └── tags.md.jinja
v}

One command renders all six templates into [site/]:

{v
site/
├── health.md
├── hydration.md
├── index.md
├── plan.md
├── shopping.md
└── tags.md
v}

A vault does not have to be flat — [note.dir] and [note.segments] exist so a
template can group a nested vault by subtree — but a flat one keeps this
example about the wiring.

{1 A note}

Each note carries the frontmatter the templates filter and sort on:

{v
---
title: Sourdough Loaf
tags: [bread, weekend]
flour: 500
created: 2026-01-15
---
# Sourdough Loaf

A long, slow bake. Build the levain from [[starter]] the night before, then
follow the folds in [[kneading]].
v}

Only [tags] and [created] are read by name; everything else arrives under
[note.frontmatter], which is how [flour] reaches a template. A note with no
[title] falls back to its first heading, and [focaccia.md] writes its tags the
inline way ([tags: bread, quick]) to show both forms are accepted.

{1 The rule}

Rendering is one rule, whatever the vault contains:

{v
(rule
 (targets (dir site))
 (deps (source_tree vault) hydration.json shopping.json)
 (action
  (run %{bin:oyster} build vault -o site
    -context hydration.json -context shopping.json)))
v}

{2 [(source_tree vault)]}

A template that queries the whole vault depends on every note in it. The
dependency is coarse on purpose: editing any note re-renders every page.
Fine-grained dependencies would require knowing which notes a template actually
read, which the engine does not report — a Jinja expression selects notes at
run time.

It is also correct, which a narrower dependency would not be. Adding a link to
[focaccia.md] changes the count printed for [kneading.md], and the rule reruns.

{2 A directory target}

[(targets (dir site))] needs [(using directory-targets 0.1)] in [dune-project];
without it the stanza is a parse error. Only one rule may write a given
directory, which matters below, where an intermediate render needs a directory
of its own.

{2 A named alias}

[minijinja-cli] is not an OCaml dependency; [oyster] resolves it from [PATH],
and the code-block rules below reach for [uv] the same way. Attaching the site
to its own alias rather than to [@default] keeps a build that never renders
from needing either binary.

{1 Generated pages are notes}

[oyster build] renders every template against the vault, indexes the results as
notes, and renders again, stopping when a pass changes nothing. So a template
may describe a page in terms of pages other templates produce, to any depth,
and nothing declares an order.

That is visible in the note count. Four notes are on disk; ten are in the
context, because the six rendered pages joined them:

{v
# Recipe index

10 notes, newest first.

- [Sourdough Loaf](sourdough.md) — bread, weekend
- [Focaccia](focaccia.md) — bread, quick
- [Starter](starter.md) — reference
- [Kneading](kneading.md) — technique, reference
- [Vault health](health.md)
- [Hydration](hydration.md)
- [Recipe index](index.md)
- [Bake plan](plan.md)
- [Shopping list](shopping.md)
- [Tags](tags.md)
v}

Generated pages are held in memory between passes and never written into the
vault, so every pass renders against the same source tree. Only the settled
pass reaches the filesystem, under [-o].

{2 Templates that never settle}

A template set that describes itself can fail to have a fixed point, and this
one nearly does. [health.md.jinja] lists the notes nothing links to. Written
the obvious way, it links to them:

{v
{% for p in orphans -%}
- [{{ notes_by_path[p].title }}]({{ p }})
{% endfor -%}
v}

Then [index.md], having nothing pointing at it, is listed as an orphan — and
that listing is a link, so on the next pass [index.md] is no longer an orphan
and drops off, so on the pass after that it is one again. The report changes
what it reports. [oyster build] renders to [-max-passes] and then fails naming
the page:

{v
no fixed point after 10 passes; still changing: health.md
v}

The fix is to break the feedback rather than to raise the limit — name the
paths without linking to them:

{v
{% for p in orphans -%}
- `{{ p }}` — {{ notes_by_path[p].title }}
{% endfor -%}
v}

A report that points at what it measures is worth a second look in general;
here the build refuses to guess which of the two answers you meant.

{1 Templates}

List every note newest first, annotated with its tags:

{v
# Recipe index

{{ vault.note_count }} notes, newest first.

{% for n in notes | sort(attribute="created", reverse=true) -%}
- [{{ n.title }}]({{ n.path }})
{%- if n.tags %} — {{ n.tags | join(", ") }}{% endif %}
{% endfor %}
v}

Drive an index off the tag map instead, resolving each path through
[notes_by_path]:

{v
{% for tag, paths in tags | items -%}
## {{ tag }} ({{ paths | length }})

{% for p in paths -%}
- [{{ notes_by_path[p].title }}]({{ p }})
{% endfor %}
{% endfor -%}
v}

{v
## bread (2)

- [Focaccia](focaccia.md)
- [Sourdough Loaf](sourdough.md)

## quick (1)

- [Focaccia](focaccia.md)
...
v}

Or report on the graph rather than listing it:

{v
{{ vault.note_count }} notes · {{ vault.link_count }} links · {{ vault.unresolved_count }} unresolved

## Links pointing nowhere

{% for u in unresolved -%}
- `{{ u.source }}`:{{ u.line }} → `{{ u.target }}` ({{ u.reason }})
{% endfor %}
v}

{v
10 notes · 24 links · 1 unresolved

## Links pointing nowhere

- `starter.md`:7 → `hydration-table` (missing_path)
v}

{1 Filtering}

Selecting on a tag is a membership test on the note's [tags], not a
[selectattr] against a list:

{v
{% for n in notes | selectattr("created") | sort(attribute="created", reverse=true) %}
{% if "bread" in n.tags %}- {{ n.created }} — [{{ n.title }}]({{ n.path }})
{% endif %}{% endfor %}
v}

[selectattr("tags", "in", \["bread"\])] silently matches nothing: it tests
whether the whole [tags] list is an element of [\["bread"\]]. Sorting on
[created] works directly because the dates are ISO-8601 strings — see
{!page-"template-context".dates}.

{1 Executing a code block}

A note can own a computation as well as prose. [oyster block] queries the blocks
of a note and writes what it finds to stdout; with [-content] it writes what a
container holds rather than its source, so a code block arrives without its
fence and the build system can pipe it wherever it belongs — here, into [uv]:

{v
{#hydration}
```python
import json

flour, water, salt = 500, 350, 10
print(json.dumps({"hydration": {"flour": flour, "water": water, ...}}))
```
v}

{v
oyster block NOTE -id ID [-lang INFO] -content
v}

[-lang] asserts rather than selects: it fails if the block's info string is not
[INFO], so a rule cannot quietly feed a shell script to a Python interpreter. A
query that matches nothing is an error too, so a rule fails rather than feeding
an interpreter an empty string. Blocks can also be addressed by heading,
kind and position, and printed as JSON.

[oyster build] runs no code. It renders, and [-context FILE] merges a JSON file
into the namespace the templates read — repeatably, and forwarded untouched to
[minijinja-cli], which already accepts several data files. What produces those
files, and under what interpreter, stays in [dune]:

{v
(rule
 (targets hydration.json)
 (deps vault/sourdough.md)
 (action
  (with-stdout-to
   hydration.json
   (pipe-stdout
    (run %{bin:oyster} block vault/sourdough.md -id hydration -lang python -content)
    (run uv run -)))))
v}

The template then reads [hydration] beside the vault's own namespace, and
editing the numbers in the note changes the rendered page on the next build.
Note the dependency: one file. Editing an unrelated note does not rerun the
interpreter.

{2 A block in a generated note}

[shopping.md.jinja] writes a note that {e contains} a code block, built from the
vault's own frontmatter:

{v
{{ "{#shopping}" }}
```python
loaves = {
{%- for n in notes %}{% if n.frontmatter.flour %}
    "{{ n.title }}": {{ n.frontmatter.flour }},
{%- endif %}{% endfor %}
}
v}

That block cannot be extracted before it exists, and it exists only once the
vault has been rendered. So the site is rendered twice: once into [site1/],
whose only purpose is to hold a page to extract from, and once into [site/].

{v
(rule
 (targets (dir site1))
 (deps (source_tree vault) hydration.json shopping-stub.json)
 (action
  (run %{bin:oyster} build vault -o site1
    -context hydration.json -context shopping-stub.json)))

(rule
 (targets shopping.json)
 (deps site1/shopping.md)
 (action
  (with-stdout-to
   shopping.json
   (pipe-stdout
    (run %{bin:oyster} block site1/shopping.md -id shopping -lang python -content)
    (run uv run -)))))
v}

A rule may depend on one file inside a directory target, which is what
[site1/shopping.md] is. Nothing else from [site1/] is used: the final render
recomputes every page from the vault.

The chain's length is written by hand, and it is the depth at which generated
code depends on generated code — one extra render per level. A block inside a
page generated by the {e final} render would need a third, and you would notice
because the block would arrive in [site/] unexpanded.

{2 [shopping-stub.json]}

[plan.md.jinja] reads [shopping.total_flour], which does not exist during the
first render. MiniJinja tolerates a missing key on a map — it renders empty —
but reading an attribute of a name that is not bound at all is an error, even
without [--strict]. [shopping-stub.json] is the two-byte file that binds it:

{v
{"shopping": {}}
v}

It is passed only to the render whose pages are discarded.

{2 Addressing by id, not by position}

"The third Python block" is a reference into a document people edit: insert an
example above it and the build quietly runs something else. An id is stable
under editing, and it is the same anchor the rest of the vault already uses —
the LSP resolves it, renames it, and reports it when it breaks.

{2 [{#id}] is a Jinja comment}

Djot's attribute syntax collides with Jinja's comment delimiters: a template
containing a literal [{#hydration}] opens a comment that is never closed, and
the render fails with a syntax error. Quote it to write it out:

{v
Computed by running the {{ "{#hydration}" }} block in ...
v}

A template that emits a code block for a later round to run, as
[shopping.md.jinja] does, needs this for the id it is writing out.

{2 What you are agreeing to}

The vault becomes trusted input: building it runs code that any note can
contain. And [dune] caches the result, so a script that reads the clock, the
network, or the environment makes the build depend on something the build
cannot see. Keep executed blocks deterministic, or accept that the page is a
snapshot of whenever it last ran.
